\b Methodology: Adaptive Data Mesh Governance Using ADGRI\b0\par
\par
This study implements an adaptive governance-scoring framework for Data Mesh domains using ADGRI (Adaptive Domain Governance Reliability Index). Governance is evaluated per domain across volume, freshness, and distribution instability under batch governance cycles.\par
\par
\b 1) Domain Dataset Construction\b0\par
Silver-layer datasets are mapped to domain-specific datasets through a reload pipeline. Each pipeline cycle copies Silver CSV inputs into domain output locations and records execution metadata (run_id, per-domain row count, status, timing). Domain outputs are persisted as per-domain CSV files and serve as the primary state for governance evaluation.\par
\par
\b 2) Governance Data Inputs\b0\par
For each domain, governance computation uses domain output datasets (latest domain CSV), monitoring history time series (domain_health_history.csv), pipeline execution logs (pipeline_log.json), and file metadata fallback when temporal signals are sparse. Extracted fields include domain_name, row_count, timestamp, freshness_hours, rows_processed, status, and domain-specific date/numeric columns.\par
\par
\b 3) Volume Stability Modeling\b0\par
Volume deviation is computed from latest row count against rolling baseline: z_v = |x_latest - mu_v| / sigma_v, with a safe denominator fallback when sigma_v is approximately zero. Higher z_v indicates stronger volume irregularity.\par
\par
\b 4) Freshness Stability Modeling\b0\par
Freshness lag is primarily computed from business-date recency: L_f = max(0, (t_eval - t_latest_business)/3600). If business-date context is insufficient, refresh-interval behavior is used; if timestamps are sparse, file-age heuristics are applied.\par
\par
\b 5) Distribution Stability Modeling\b0\par
Date-qualified numeric non-ID columns are used. Daily feature vectors are built from per-feature mean and variance. Drift is measured via per-feature z-scores and aggregated as z_d = sqrt((1/k) * sum(z_i^2)), where k is the number of valid feature statistics.\par
\par
\b 6) Deviation-to-Risk Transformation\b0\par
For each factor i in {v, f, d}, risk is derived as r_i = erf(max(0, z_i)/sqrt(2)), yielding bounded instability risk in [0,1].\par
\par
\b 7) Confidence Estimation\b0\par
Confidence is computed per factor using sample sufficiency and variability attenuation: c_i = clip_[0,1]((log(1+n_i)/log(1+W)) * (1/(1+variability_i))), where n_i is baseline sample size and W is rolling window size.\par
\par
\b 8) Dynamic Confidence-Aware Weighting\b0\par
Weights are confidence-normalized: w_i = c_i / (c_v + c_f + c_d), with numerical flooring for stable normalization.\par
\par
\b 9) ADGRI Formulation\b0\par
Weighted governance risk: R_adgri = w_v*r_v + w_f*r_f + w_d*r_d. Final score: ADGRI = 100 * (1 - R_adgri). Output range is [0,100], where higher values indicate stronger governance reliability.\par
\par
\b 10) Trend and Explanation Layer\b0\par
The system computes governance trend over recent refresh cycles, classifies trend direction (improving/stable/deteriorating), and provides contribution-based explanation of dominant instability factors.\par
\par
\b 11) Operational Integration and Triggers\b0\par
Governance is recomputed after pipeline refresh operations and in controlled admin workflows (scenario execution, baseline restoration, correction-and-rerun). Computation fuses latest domain state, historical monitoring context, and pipeline logs.\par
\par
\b 12) System Outputs\b0\par
Outputs include per-domain ADGRI, weighted risk, confidence score/level, trend direction/slope, contribution breakdown, cross-domain rankings, prioritization recommendations, and pipeline health alerts.\par
\par
\b 13) Control and Experimental Validation\b0\par
Controlled validation injects prepared or uploaded test datasets into mapped Silver targets, triggers rerun, recomputes governance, and compares before/after ADGRI and factor-level risk deltas (freshness, volume, distribution), including reason-label and business-date shifts.\par
